<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tahirid dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tahirids</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tahirid dynasty was a line of governors who ruled Khurasan in the ninth century, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahir-ibn-husayn">Tahir ibn Husayn</a>, a general of the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>. Although they acknowledged Abbasid authority, the Tahirids governed with considerable hereditary autonomy. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> list them among the successor dynasties to which military and political power devolved in the eastern provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tahirid dynasty" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arab League · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tahirid_Dynasty_821_-_873_(AD).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tahirids were a culturally Arabized, Sunni Muslim dynasty of Persian dehqan origin. Their founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahir-ibn-husayn">Tahir ibn Husayn</a>, played a major military role in the civil war between the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amin">al-Amin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a>, and he and his ancestors had earlier been given minor governorships in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> for their service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. In 821 Tahir was made governor of Khorasan, but he died soon afterwards, and the caliph appointed his son Talha. Another son, Abdallah, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to retake the province from a rebel and was made governor there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Talha failed to remove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharijites">Kharijites</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, and the Tahirids occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaranj">Zarang</a> without ever collecting taxes in the surrounding countryside. Abdallah took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> when Talha died in 828. His reign saw a flourishing of agriculture in Khorasan, and he was a noted poet. The historian Hugh Kennedy rejects the view that the Tahirids were the first independent Iranian dynasty and calls the arrangement a partnership between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> and the Tahirids.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-tahir">Muhammad ibn Tahir</a> the dynasty declined. Oppressive policies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> led its people to revolt and declare allegiance to the Zaydi ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd">Hasan ibn Zayd</a> in 864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a> had already been lost to rebels. In 873 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarid dynasty</a> overthrew Muhammad and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The Tahirids also served as military governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from 820. Ishaq ibn Ibrahim held the city for over twenty-five years and carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihna">Mihna</a> there. In the 860s Abdallah, a later governor of the city, gave refuge to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Musta'in during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchy-at-samarra">Anarchy at Samarra</a> and commanded Baghdad's defence when al-Mu'tazz besieged it in 865. In 891 Badr al-Mu'tadidi took charge of the city's security in place of the Tahirids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Although Persian, the Tahirids were eager to be accepted in the caliphal world, where Arabic culture carried prestige. They patronised Arabic poetry and did not take part in the renaissance of Early New Persian, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> helped lead. Later writers such as Daulatshah said that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-tahir">Abdallah ibn Tahir</a> ordered Persian works destroyed. The historian Shivan Mahendrarajah credits the Tahirids with starting the process by which Persian came to be written in the Perso-Arabic script.</p><p>They supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> orthodoxy and suppressed sectarian movements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, and their coins and sermons acknowledged the Abbasid caliphs. The religious scholar Abu Ubayd dedicated his Garibu'l-Hadis to Abdallah. Mansur ibn Talha rebuilt the minaret of the congregational mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tahirid dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Military power passed increasingly to provincial commanders and then to outright successor dynasties: the <b>Tahirids</b>, Saffarids and Samanids in the east, and later the Buyids and Seljuks at the centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 864 the people of Ruyan and Tabaristan, angered by land seizures under <b>Tahirid</b> governors, invited Hasan ibn Zayd, a descendant of the Prophet's grandson Hasan, to lead them.</blockquote><a href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
